      <ModuleCard name="stream" color={T.teal} icon="🌊" tagline="Process data chunk by chunk — essential for large files and HTTP">
        <EasyBox emoji="🌊" title="What are streams and why do they exist?" color={T.teal}>
          Imagine you need to read a 4GB log file and count how many times the word "ERROR" appears. Without streams, your program would load the entire 4GB into RAM, then count. Most servers don't have 4GB free. The program crashes.<br /><br />
          Streams solve this by processing data <strong>one chunk at a time</strong> (~64KB by default). Your program uses ~100KB of RAM regardless of whether the file is 1MB or 10GB. It's like reading a book one page at a time instead of photocopying the entire book before starting.<br /><br />
          <strong>Important:</strong> HTTP requests and responses in Node.js are already streams. File reads can be streams. This isn't a niche feature — it's central to how Node handles data.
        </EasyBox>
        <CodeBlock label="4 types of streams" code={`// READABLE stream — you read FROM it (data flows to you):
// Examples: fs.createReadStream(), http req, process.stdin

// WRITABLE stream — you write TO it (data flows away):
// Examples: fs.createWriteStream(), http res, process.stdout

// DUPLEX stream — you can read AND write:
// Examples: net.Socket (TCP connection)

// TRANSFORM stream — reads input, transforms it, outputs result:
// Examples: zlib.createGzip() (compress), crypto cipher, CSV parser`} />
        <CodeBlock label="readable stream — chunk by chunk" code={`import fs from 'fs';

const stream = fs.createReadStream('./huge-file.csv', {
  encoding: 'utf8',
  highWaterMark: 64 * 1024, // chunk size: 64KB at a time
});

let lineCount = 0;

stream.on('data', (chunk) => {
  // Called repeatedly — once per 64KB chunk
  lineCount += chunk.split('\n').length - 1;
  // Processing starts immediately, not after full file loads
});

stream.on('end', () => {
  console.log(\`Total lines: \${lineCount}\`);
  // Only ~64KB RAM used, regardless of file size
});

stream.on('error', (err) => {
  console.error('Stream error:', err.message);
});`} />
        <CodeBlock label="pipe — chain streams together (powerful!)" code={`import fs from 'fs';
import { createGzip } from 'zlib';
import { pipeline } from 'stream';
import { promisify } from 'util';

const pipe = promisify(pipeline); // makes pipeline promise-based

// SIMPLE PIPE — read, compress, write:
fs.createReadStream('./data.json')
  .pipe(createGzip())                      // compress
  .pipe(fs.createWriteStream('./data.json.gz')); // write

// ✅ Better: use pipeline() — it handles errors properly!
// pipe() doesn't clean up streams on error → memory leak
await pipe(
  fs.createReadStream('./data.json'),      // read
  createGzip(),                            // compress
  fs.createWriteStream('./data.json.gz')   // write
);
// If ANY stream errors → all streams destroyed, promise rejects

// Process a 10GB file:
await pipe(
  fs.createReadStream('./10gb-export.csv'),
  new TransformCSVtoJSON(),  // your custom Transform stream
  fs.createWriteStream('./output.json')
);
// RAM used: ~200KB total. Not 10GB. ✅`} />
        <CodeBlock label="custom transform stream — modify data in-flight" code={`import { Transform } from 'stream';

// A Transform that converts each line to uppercase:
class UpperCase extends Transform {
  _transform(chunk, encoding, callback) {
    // chunk = one piece of data (Buffer or string)
    this.push(chunk.toString().toUpperCase()); // push transformed data downstream
    callback(); // call when ready for next chunk
  }

  _flush(callback) {
    // Called when all data has been processed
    // Good place for cleanup or final output
    callback();
  }
}

// Use it in a pipeline:
await pipe(
  fs.createReadStream('./input.txt'),
  new UpperCase(),
  fs.createWriteStream('./output.txt')
);`} />
